   "source": [
    "<div style=\"text-align:center; margin:30px auto; max-width:900px;\">\n",
    "\n",
    "<h2>\n",
    "An analysis of the propensity of Large Language Models to generate programs with software vulnerabilities\n",
    "</h2>\n",
    "\n",
    "</div>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "8db6335b",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# Introduction"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "13deb2d3",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "This report analyses a dataset of C programs generated by Large Language Models (LLMs). Each row corresponds to one generated program and contains information about the generating model, program size, cyclomatic complexity, and whether the program was classified as vulnerable.\n",
    "\n",
    "The main research question is:\n",
    "\n",
    "> **Are some LLMs less likely to generate vulnerable C code, and are code-size/complexity measures associated with vulnerability?**\n",
    "\n",
    "The analysis investigates three hypotheses:\n",
    "\n",
    "- **H1:** `llama2` is less likely to produce programs classified as `VULNERABLE` than the other LLMs.\n",
    "- **H2:** Cyclomatic complexity is associated with the number of lines of code.\n",
    "- **H3:** The number of lines of code increases the probability that a generated program is classified as `VULNERABLE`.\n",
    "\n",
    "All hypotheses are analysed using Bayesian regression models. For binary vulnerability outcomes, I use logistic regression. For cyclomatic complexity, I use a Gaussian linear regression model after standardising the predictor variables."
   ]
  },
