    "### 7. Plot: vulnerable vs non-vulnerable per LLM"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "290f46ad",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# Hypothesis 1 "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "cda855db",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## The LLM llama2 is less likely to produce programs with vulnerabilities than any other LLM in the dataset."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "28c3b5e4",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Bayesian modelling notation used in this report\n",
    "\n",
    "For binary outcomes, the response variable is:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "y_i = \\begin{cases}\n",
    "1, & \\text{if program } i \\text{ is classified as VULNERABLE} \\\\\n",
    "0, & \\text{otherwise}\n",
    "\\end{cases}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "The logistic regression models use:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "y_i \\sim \\text{Bernoulli}(p_i)\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\text{logit}(p_i) = \\log \\left(\\frac{p_i}{1-p_i}\\right) = \\eta_i\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Equivalently:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "p_i = \\frac{1}{1 + e^{-\\eta_i}}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "This is useful because probabilities must stay between 0 and 1, while the linear predictor $\\eta_i$ can take any real value. In plain language: the model does not predict vulnerability probability directly with a straight line, because straight lines can go below 0 or above 1. Logistic regression keeps the prediction inside the probability range, like putting bumpers on a bowling lane."
   ]
  },
